---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { seo } from '../lib/content';
import { defaultSeo } from '../lib/blocks';
import { DOORS } from '../consts';

const door = DOORS[1];

// Editable from the joshpenzell.com admin; falls back to the default above.
const meta = await seo('ia.seo.advisory', defaultSeo('ia.seo.advisory'));
---

<Base title={meta.title} description={meta.description} path="/executive-advisory/">
  <section class="wrap page-head">
    <p class="eyebrow">Ways to work together</p>
    <h1>Executive advisory</h1>
    <p class="lede">{door.for}</p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap page-body">
    <p>
      <strong>Outcome.</strong>
      {door.outcome}
    </p>

    <div class="section-rule">
      <h2>What this is for</h2>
      <p>
        Consequential decisions often arrive on an executive's desk disguised as smaller
        questions: Which platform? Which team goes first? What should the program say?
        Which idea deserves funding? Those are real questions, and they are often not the
        decision that matters.
      </p>
      <p>
        The decision that matters is what the work is actually about—and therefore what
        should change, what should not, and what you would need to see to believe it is
        working. Advisory work is where that gets settled before budget follows it.
      </p>
      <p class="pull">Do not spend beautifully against the wrong question.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="section-rule">
      <h2>How it runs</h2>
      <p>
        Advisory engagements are shaped around your calendar rather than a fixed
        curriculum. In practice they tend to involve:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Interpretation.</strong> Working through the choice in front of you and naming
          what it is really a choice between.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Direction.</strong> Getting the leadership team into the same play, in language
          the rest of the organization can act on.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Experiment design.</strong> Turning the direction into a small number of bounded
          attempts with a stated cost, a stated duration, and an agreed way to read the result.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Interpretation again.</strong> Reading what actually happened, including the
          parts that contradict the plan.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The output is not a deck of recommendations. It is a set of decisions you can
        defend and a rehearsal plan for the ones you cannot make yet.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="section-rule">
      <h2>What we will tell you</h2>
      <p>
        If the evidence does not support a move, we will say so. Our published research
        exists partly to hold ourselves to that standard: it states its own limitations in
        the body of the work, and it says plainly where a result does not establish cause.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href="/publications/">See the research →</a>
      </p>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="btn" href="/contact/">Start a conversation</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
